#{[17592186455441 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186455407 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455441 #edb/ref 1069 "q-80451" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455441 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455441 #edb/ref 1071 "Determine if the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]$ \nis diagonalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455441 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3a4040a9-af29-5df2-8d92-a7f8acbeb04b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455441 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}3-\\lambda  & -4 \\\\ 2 & -3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )(-3-\\lambda )-2\\cdot (-4) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)(\\lambda -1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$ Since we get two distinct eigenvalues, the matrix $A$ is diagonalizable.\n\nNow, we construct the diagonal matrix $D,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455468 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186455343 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455468 #edb/ref 1069 "q-80390" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455468 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455468 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the matrix $B$ for which ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-6$ are eigenvalues, and\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nare the respective eigenvectors. What are the values of $a,b,c$ given that\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ b & c\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & a\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ b & c\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}?\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455468 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6fd075d3-5c0d-5ed0-b894-521c86e9d041" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455468 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-6,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=4,$ and\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-6.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn conclusion, we get  $a=-6,$ $b=3,$ and $c=-1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455503 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186455379 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455503 #edb/ref 1069 "q-80391" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455503 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455503 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the matrix $E=[\\begin{aligned}-32 & 40 \\\\ -30 & 38\\end{aligned}].$ You're given that ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=8$ are the eigenvalues of $E$, and\n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$, ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ are the respective eigenvectors. Find the matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PD{P}^{-1}=E,$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455503 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dde0c5ba-d69f-5523-84e3-29d85ec2262a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455503 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=8,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2,$ and\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=8.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
